# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout by default. Every file here
# that is read by a Linux shell inside the container then arrives with a
# trailing carriage return, and `set -e\r` is not `set -e` — the shell reports
# "Illegal option -", the entrypoint dies on its first line, and the container
# restarts forever while `docker compose` cheerfully reports "Started".
#
# Without this file that failure depends on which machine cloned the repo,
# which is the worst kind of bug to be handed: it works for the person who
# wrote it and nowhere else.

# Normalise everything to LF in the repository and on checkout. Windows editors
# have handled LF for years; Linux shells have never handled CR.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Said again explicitly for the files where getting it wrong stops the clinic
# system from starting at all.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf
docker/*    text eol=lf

# Except Windows batch files, which are the one thing that genuinely wants CRLF:
# cmd.exe can mis-parse a LF-only .bat, and these are the files the clinic
# double-clicks every morning.
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.xlsx      binary
*.xlsm      binary
